# SECTION 5 — OBSERVABLES & DETECTION ARTIFACTS

## 5A — STATIC / NETWORK IOCs

Concrete observables: IPv4/IPv6, domains, hostnames, URLs, hashes, filenames, file paths, registry keys/values, mutexes, named pipes, service names, scheduled-task names, certificates, User-Agent strings, email addresses, cryptocurrency addresses, other campaign-specific identifiers.

| Value | Type | Provenance | Context / Role | Evidence | Operational Action | False-Positive Risk |
| :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- | :---- |

**Operational Action:** `BLOCK / HUNT / DETECT / ENRICH / CONTEXT ONLY / DO NOT OPERATIONALIZE`

**Specificity gate (apply before listing):** a filename, file path, registry key, or service name qualifies as a static IOC **only if it is attacker- or campaign-specific**. Generic OS artifacts touched during reconnaissance — e.g. `HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion`, `HKLM\...\Cryptography\MachineGuid`, standard system paths — are **not** static IOCs; place the associated activity in 5C as behavior, or mark it `CONTEXT ONLY`. Do not classify documentation URLs, legitimate cloud-provider endpoints, security-vendor resources, crawler IP ranges, or public APIs as malicious. When the report lists a value so defenders can ALLOW/VERIFY/distinguish it, classify it accordingly rather than as malicious.

## 5B — COMMAND-LINE IOCs (HUNT-READY)

Include an attacker command as a standalone command-line IOC only when **all** hold:

1. it appears explicitly in the source;  
2. it is attributed to attacker activity;  
3. it is a **process command line**, not multi-statement script source;  
4. the report supplies a **hunt discriminator** distinguishing the malicious instance from routine administration — at least one of: parent process, execution path, **argument anomaly** (suspicious on argument shape alone, e.g. a token-gated DLL export), or position in a tasked sequence.

For each qualifying command: **Command:** `<verbatim; report placeholders/elisions preserved; no added interpreter>` **Provenance:** `body text / Figure N` (add `[FROM IMAGE]` only if inside a figure) **Value Type:** `exact-match / generalized pattern` **Binary / Interpreter:** name each binary and classify — `LOLBin (LOLBAS-cataloged)` / `native OS binary/interpreter` (e.g. `powershell.exe`, `cmd.exe`, `wscript.exe`, `reg.exe`, `net.exe` — do not assert LOLBAS unless truly listed) / `third-party binary` (e.g. `node.exe` — state it is not an OS binary) **Hunt Discriminator:** the source-supported anchor(s); cite the report, do not invent **Detection Fidelity:** `HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW` **Hunt Logic:** `[ANALYST-DERIVED]` vendor-neutral pseudo-query combining the command with its discriminator **Why It Matters:** source-grounded; avoid absolutes **MITRE ATT\&CK:** `T####.### — name`

**Posture wiring (Command Capture Posture):**

- Under **DROP LOW-FIDELITY** (default): commands assessed `Detection Fidelity: LOW` are removed from 5B and represented as behavior in 5C. Only MEDIUM/HIGH standalone commands remain here.  
- Under **KEEP-AND-TAG**: all attacker command lines remain in 5B with their fidelity tag; none are dropped.

A URL inside a command stays part of the command, but also extract it into 5A when appropriate.

If none: `Not identified in the report.`

## 5C — BEHAVIORAL / HUNTING-ONLY PATTERNS

Activity that does not qualify as a standalone command IOC: reconnaissance bursts; multi-statement PowerShell; shell/JS/VBS loaders and script bodies; unusual process ancestry; LOLBin argument combinations; service-creation patterns; network connections from unusual processes (e.g. WinRM to `:5985/wsman`); credential-access sequences; remote-service use; sanitized/representative commands; attacker task sequences.

For each: **Behavior:** · **Observed Evidence:** · **Required Telemetry:** · **Detection Logic:** `[ANALYST-DERIVED]` · **Detection Fidelity:** `HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW` · **MITRE ATT\&CK:** · **False-Positive Considerations:**

When several ordinary commands become meaningful only because they share a malicious parent or run in a tight attacker-tasking sequence, create **one** behavioral detection rather than several low-value command IOCs. Commands explicitly labeled "sanitized/representative/example" belong here as detection logic (give the huntable substring set), never in 5A/5B as observed IOCs.

